
########## queries.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

       10  [P] from the preprints
       14  [V] verified against a named source
        5  [A] assumed range, swept

      Hardware figures are the RTX 3090 the author's own preprints
      specify, from the catalogue.  No GPU is needed to run this.

1. The claimed rate against the card, for each node size

  [P] "millions of physical queries per millisecond" = 1,000,000,000 /s and up

      4x4 node  (32 B, 128 FLOP per MMA)
        compute ceiling             1,109,375,000,000 queries/s
        bandwidth ceiling              14,625,000,000 queries/s
        binding                        14,625,000,000 queries/s   (bandwidth)
        vs the claim                            14.62 x   the claim FITS

      112x112 node  (25,088 B, 2,809,856 FLOP per MMA)
        compute ceiling                    50,536,398 queries/s
        bandwidth ceiling                  18,654,337 queries/s
        binding                            18,654,337 queries/s   (bandwidth)
        vs the claim                             0.02 x   the claim EXCEEDS the card

      So the claim is true for the 4x4 node with 14x of headroom, and
      false by 54x for the 112x112 node, and Sec. 5 makes it for both at
      once.  Worth stating plainly: half of this claim is correct.

2. But the node sizes do not match the hardware's tile

  [V] tensor-core MMA granularity      16x16
        4x4   node needs 1x1 tile(s), occupancy   6.2%
      112x112 node needs 7x7 tile(s), occupancy 100.0%

      A 4x4 node fills one sixteenth of a 16x16 tile, so the compute
      ceiling above is optimistic by that factor unless many nodes are
      packed into one tile -- which is possible, but is a different
      algorithm from the one-node-one-MMA model Sec. 3 describes.  112 is
      also not a multiple of 16, so it needs 7x7 tiles at 100% only by
      coincidence of 112 = 7 x 16.  That one is exact.

3. The rate against the application it is claimed for

  [P] IPC Sec. 4: a cast ray "interrogates" the local node at each step,
      so every march step is a query.

      [A] target 1920x1080 at 60 Hz = 124,416,000 pixel-frames/s

       steps/pixel     queries/s needed   vs the claim
                32        3,981,312,000          3.98x
                64        7,962,624,000          7.96x
               128       15,925,248,000         15.93x
               256       31,850,496,000         31.85x

      the claimed rate buys                8.0 march steps per pixel

      Volumetric ray marching normally wants 64 to 256 samples per pixel,
      so the claimed rate is 4 to 32 times short of its own application --
      and that is before any of those steps does physics, writes a result,
      or reads the ray state.  The rate is simultaneously achievable in the
      abstract and insufficient for the purpose it is quoted for.

4. What the paper's own rendering reference cost

  [V] NeRF, reference [4] of both preprints:
      queries per rendered image          150 - 200 million
      time per frame on a V100           30.0 s   = 0.033 frames/s

      So the work cited as the predecessor for "absolute real-time"
      volumetric synthesis ran at about 0.03 frames per second.  Its query
      count per image is in the same range this script computes as
      necessary, which is a point in the preprint's favour: the
      architecture's estimate of how much work a frame takes is right.
      What it gets wrong is how fast that work can be done.

5. The word that got stronger between versions

  [P] openQL v1.0 Sec. 2.3 said the update "preserves the Lie group symmetries"
  [P] IPC Sec. 3 calls it "a symplectic discrete Hamiltonian operator"   True

      The expression is unchanged:  S(t+1) = sigma(W_PINN (x) S(t) + b)

  [V] "symplectic" occurs in 1 work cited by
      either preprint -- Baez, about the 56-dimensional Freudenthal space
      for E7 -- and not in connection with a time step.

      This claim was already measured in openql-eval/symplectic.py and the
      numbers are reproduced there, not here, to avoid duplicating a
      measurement: with sigma = tanh a harmonic oscillator drifts to
      2e-5 x E(0) over 1e5 steps, and a W fitted to exact pendulum data
      reaches 10.2 x E(0), while leapfrog holds 1.0000 x E(0) untrained.
      A nonlinearity applied after an affine map is not a symplectic map;
      stating it more strongly in a later paper does not change that.

########## bldc.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

       10  [P] from the preprints
       14  [V] verified against a named source
        5  [A] assumed range, swept

1. What one evaluation costs

  [P] 12x12 fp16 node                             288 B
  [P] million-node grid, state only                274.7 MiB
      fits in 24 GiB                                    89 such grids
      one full update sweep moves                  549.3 MiB
      time for one sweep at 936 GB/s                0.615 ms

      This is a bandwidth bound, not a FLOP bound, so it is the floor on
      what a sweep can cost however the arithmetic is arranged.

2. How many sweeps "millions per day" leaves

        topologies/day      time each   sweeps affordable
             1,000,000      86.400 ms              140.4
             2,000,000      43.200 ms               70.2
             5,000,000      17.280 ms               28.1
            10,000,000       8.640 ms               14.0

      So the claim requires a converged field solution in 14 to
      140 sweeps of the whole grid, depending on how many millions
      is meant.

      For scale: a multigrid Poisson solve on a 100^3 grid is in that
      range for ONE linear solve.  And the objective function Sec. 6.1
      describes is not one linear solve -- it has to evaluate, at minimum:
        - the magnetostatic field of the proposed winding
        - the resulting torque and its ripple over a full electrical cycle
        - cogging torque, which is a function of rotor position
        - iron loss, which needs the B-H history at every node
        - the thermal steady state, which needs the loss field as input

      Each of those is at least one solve, several are coupled, and the
      position-dependent ones need the field recomputed at many rotor
      angles.  The budget is one solve's worth of sweeps for all of it.

3. What the zero-branching argument actually buys

  [P] "computing empty space takes the exact same nanosecond fraction as
      computing a highly complex, asymmetric copper coil"

      This is TRUE, and it is the one part of the argument that survives
      intact.  An MMA pipeline does not skip work, so cost is independent
      of content.  But that is a statement that there is no SAVING on empty
      space, not that there is a speed advantage -- and a conventional
      solver's main economy on a motor grid is precisely that most of the
      volume is air:

        grid empty   sparse solver does   dense does    penalty
              50%               50.0%        100%       2.0x
              70%               30.0%        100%       3.3x
              90%               10.0%        100%      10.0x
              99%                1.0%        100%     100.0x

      So zero-branching is the price of the architecture, not its
      advantage.  Sec. 6.1 presents the absence of a saving as the thing
      that "circumvents" the combinatorial limit.

4. And the combinatorial claim is not about the grid at all

  [P] "Applying blind brute-force to a million-node grid yields an
      insurmountable combinatorial explosion."

      The explosion is in the SEARCH SPACE of topologies, not in the cost
      of evaluating one.  A genetic algorithm addresses it by evaluating
      few candidates well, so what it needs is a cheap, accurate objective
      -- which is what Sec. 2 computes the budget for.  Making each
      evaluation branchless does not reduce the number of candidates, and
      the number of candidates is the quantity called insurmountable.

      [P] the stated inputs are a spatial volume and "48V",
      with no stated parameterisation of the winding, so the dimension of
      the search space is not given and cannot be checked here.

5. The one new physical claim in Sec. 6.1

  [P] "fractal-like winding patterns that minimize skin effect"

      Skin effect is current crowding toward a conductor's surface, and the
      established remedy is to SUBDIVIDE the conductor into strands thinner
      than the skin depth and transpose them, which is Litz wire.  A
      fractal boundary increases surface area at fixed cross-section, which
      is the opposite move: more perimeter for the same copper means more
      of the conductor sits in the high-current-density region.

      Fractal and space-filling geometries do have real uses in RF
      antennas, where a long electrical path in a small volume is wanted.
      That is a different objective from minimising AC resistance in a
      motor winding, and Sec. 6.1 gives no mechanism linking the two.
      This is recorded as an unsupported claim rather than a measured
      defect: no skin-effect calculation is attempted here, because the
      section specifies no winding geometry to compute one for.

########## versions.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

       10  [P] from the preprints
       14  [V] verified against a named source
        5  [A] assumed range, swept

      v1.0 is DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23112560, 3 October 2026.
      v1.1 is DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23241148, 8 October 2026.
      Both are reproduced by fetch.sh in the reference-audit repository.

1. Removed in v1.1

      - the derivation of 112  <- measured in openql-eval
          "The dimensionality of 112 in openOL is derived from the necessary
           degrees of freedom to encapsulate the macroscopic 12D gauge groups
           alongside the microstate variables of crystallography, spin
           configurations, and valence orbital interactions"

      - the tensor-core load figure  <- measured in openql-eval
          "keeps Tensor Core load in the efficient 15-20% range when
           simulating environments with millions of nodes"

      - the node footprint  <- measured in openql-eval
          "the memory footprint of a single node reaches tens of kilobytes"

      - the correction-loop clause  <- measured in openql-eval
          "without requiring floating-point correction loops"

      - all five reference glosses

      - the keywords 'octonions' and 'dimensional reduction'

      4 of 6 removals are claims that openql-eval
      measured and reported.  No causal claim is made: the revision has no
      changelog, and the coincidence is the whole of the evidence.

2. Added in v1.1

      + Sec. 2, extensional vs intensional computing, imported from the MPPT papers
      + Sec. 6.1, the BLDC motor case study measured in bldc.py
      + fractal-like winding patterns that minimize skin effect

3. Unchanged in v1.1

      the update rule:  S(t+1) = sigma(W_PINN (x) S(t) + b)
        v1.0 said it "preserves the Lie group symmetries"
        v1.1 keeps the same wording; the companion IPC paper of the same
        day strengthens it to "a symplectic discrete Hamiltonian
        operator" -- see queries.py Sec. 5

      the ORCID placeholder in the PDF header, still unfilled:
        v1.0  Author: Michal Mazgal [ORCID: enter-your-orcid]
        v1.1  Author: Michal Mazgal [ORCID: Zadejte-svuj-ORCID]

      The placeholder was not filled in; it was regenerated in Czech.  The
      author's real ORCID has been attached to the Zenodo record metadata
      since 3 October, so the identifier exists and only the PDF template
      is stale.

      Also unchanged: the abstract still says the architecture works "By
      transforming quaternion and octonion algebra into unified block
      matrices", while "octonions" has been dropped from the keywords.
      The octonion half of that sentence is the one excluded by a theorem,
      measured in openql-eval/octonion.py, and it is still in the abstract.

4. What the two deposits of 8 October are, relative to v1.0

      IPC (23241777) is openQL's Sec. 2, Sec. 4 and part of Sec. 5 restated
      as a standalone paper, with four of its five references shared with
      openQL and the fifth being Fitting's Stanford Encyclopedia entry on
      intensional logic.  It contains no new architecture.  What is new is
      the word 'symplectic' and the queries-per-millisecond figure, both
      measured in queries.py.

      v1.1 (23241148) is v1.0 with the six removals above, the three
      additions, and Sec. 6.1, which is the only substantively new section
      in either deposit.  It is measured in bldc.py.

      One internal inconsistency between them is worth noting: openQL puts
      12 dimensions in a 12x12 node built from three concatenated 4x4
      quaternion blocks, while IPC Sec. 3 says the node is "a 4x4 (for 12D
      macroscopic physics)" matrix -- 16 slots for 12 numbers, in a
      different arrangement from the one openQL specifies for the same
      layer.
